<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Exp Med</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Experimental Medicine</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0022-1007</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1540-9538</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">19867904</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2125307</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>THE HEART MUSCLE IN PNEUMONIA</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Newburgh</surname><given-names>L. H.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Porter</surname><given-names>W. T.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="N0x1be0c60N0x38a8598">From the Laboratory of Comparative Physiology and the Department of Medicine of the Harvard Medical School, Boston.</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>1</day><month>8</month><year>1915</year></pub-date><volume>22</volume><issue>2</issue><fpage>123</fpage><lpage>128</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>24</day><month>4</month><year>1915</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; Copyright, 1915, by The Rockefeller Institute for Medical Research New York</copyright-statement></permissions><abstract><p> 1. The heart muscle is not functionally impaired in pneumonia, since the pneumonic ventricle beats normally as soon as its food is normal. 2. Pneumonic blood, suddenly fed to normal heart muscle, lowers its efficiency, lessening the duration and the area of contraction. 3. The heart muscle in pneumonia, exposed gradually to the action of the poison, largely adjusts itself to its poisoned food. </p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>